hat exactly  they meant by transfer inflation in the  first place many people often associate  transfer inflation with a transitory  price hikes but this is not what  transfer inflation means transduring  inflation simply means that the  inflation rating itself will come down  to normal levels of two to three percent  in such a scenario the price hikes would  still be permanent it's just the time  period in which prices were being hiked  wouldn't last that long for prices to  actually go back down inflation would  not only have to come down but it would  have to go negative and we would have to  see deflation this is obviously not  something that the fed promised  hopefully that cleared up the confusion  between transfer inflation and  transferred price hikes but why did the  fed even think that inflation would be  transitory in the first place  they literally just increased the money  supply by 33 within a matter of two  years well one of the reasons the fed  believed inflation would be transitory  is temporarily surging demand you see it  the first time we saw elevated inflation  readings was in the second quarter of  2021 this was shortly after vaccines  started to become widely available  throughout america so the theory was  that economic participation rose  significantly throughout this time  period as people start to freely go to  restaurants bars hotels vacations  shopping etc this unprecedented influx  of demand would of course allow  companies to raise prices but over time  it was expected that people would pull  back spending normal levels which should  theoretically ease demand and allow  prices to stabilize aside from this  temporary surge and demand the labor  market was still rather weak throughout  the first half of 201 we primarily saw  unemployment figures in the five to six  percent range as you can see this is a  rather high reading on a historical  basis so even though some people were  getting their vaccines and spending like  crazy a good portion of america was  still struggling to get a job and make  ends meet these guys were obviously not  going to be spending a bunch of money so  it was expected that these folks would  continue to weigh down demand which  should keep inflation in check the  problem with this theory however was  that the labor market would recover  faster than anyone expected usually it  takes several years for unemployment to  drop from percent to three percent but  this time it only took a matter of  months so not only were people spending  more money but the labor market was  stronger than ever on top of these  theoretical reasons i suspect recency  bias also played a role in the fed's  perspective ever since the 2008  financial crisis inflation has been  extraordinarily low in fact throughout  much of the 2010s the fed had to fight  to prop up inflation to 2 percent  because it kept dropping towards zero  not to mention it it's been nearly 50  years since we saw high inflation so it  wouldn't be that surprising here that  the fed's perspective was skewed by  recent economic history  alright so we've discussed some of the  reasons the fed believed high inflation  would be transitory but why did they end  up being so wrong well there were a lot  of factors that were out of their  control and they simply didn't pay  enough attention to these factors such  as the supply shortage the fed along  with business leaders have been  discussing the supply shortage since the  beginning but none of them really  expected things to get this bad the  overwhelming consensus was that supply  chains were simply having a bit of  trouble getting restarted a lot of  manufacturing plants had to rehire  employees implement new covert protocols  work around lockdowns and so on and so  forth so it wasn't that surprising that  we were seeing supply chain bottlenecks  as things eased back to normal though it  was expected that these issues would  just sort themselves out but we're  actually seeing the exact opposite take  place the supply shortage is worse than  ever and economists are predicting that  this could easily last for another year  even apple who has one of the most  established supply chains in the world  was predicting sales to dive by 8  billion due to shortages and lockdowns  so not only were we seeing rapidly  recovering demand but we were seeing an  unprecedented shortage in supply and  this turned out to be a deadly  combination supply wasn't the only area  in which we saw shortages either another  major shortage we've been facing it is  the labor shortage the mass layoffs that  came at the beginning of the pandemic  caused many to learn new skills look for  higher paying jobs and make themselves  more valuable this has made it extremely  difficult for companies to find low  level labor  take disney for example they have 33 000  less employees today than they had in  2019 a similar situation can be seen as  starbucks as well as these companies  struggle to find enough labor they're  being forced to pay higher salaries and  while this is great in the short term  for employees this eventually gets  passed on to customers leading to higher  inflation  so far we've covered the surface level  reasoning behind transfer inflation the  fed thought that inflation would be  transferred due to nb but they were  wrong because of cnd  this is the overwhelming narrative from  the mainstream media and even the fed  itself but what if they weren't wrong  what if they knew that inflation would  be high for quite some time now this may  sound like a conspiracy theory but hear  me out even if the fed knew that  inflation would be high do you really  think that they would just come out and  say it that would simply cause mass  panic and the fed wants to keep more  kids and people as calm and level-headed  as possible so it makes sense that they  would try to underplay how bad inflation  would get also i know a lot of you guys  think that the people at the fed are  idiots and that they have no idea what  they're doing but do you really think  that they would have missed something so  big i mean literally half of america was  calling for high inflation if a good  portion of everyday people could see  high inflation coming i suspect vet  members with a decade of experience  could also see it coming but then why  did the fed not take any action  well the thing to keep in mind is the  fed has two roles which are to maintain  low inflation and maintain low  unemployment throughout 2020 and march  of 2021 the need to boost employment was  simply a higher priority for them than  trying to minimize inflation in other  words they were okay with letting  inflation run hot for a year or two as  long as their policies boosted the labor  market it was until unemployment  cratered this up four percent in late  2021 that inflation became their number  one priority now that they're focusing  on inflation i don't think they'll go  easy on it i mean they didn't hesitate  to go from quarter percentage rate hikes  to three quarter percentage rate hikes  in a matter of months so i don't think  they'll hesitate to go to one percent or  even higher either as long as  unemployment is low and inflation is  high they'll pump up interest rates as  much as they need to  it's great that the fed is finally  taking serious action to combat  inflation but how long will it take for  inflation to actually cool down  well there is no secret formula to  calculate this but we can take a look at  history to guide our expectations over  the past hundred years we've seen five  notable peaks of inflation four out of  these five times inflation reached  levels of over 10 percent before peaking  but in 1950 inflation peaked at around  the levels that we're at right now from  the peak it took about one year and one  month for inflation to come back down to  two percent now of course past  performance is not indicative of future  results but if we were to assume that  something like this played out right now  we could expect inflation to cool down  by august of 2023 but that's just the  best case scenario inflation could  easily run to over 10 percent like it  did four other times in history in 1941  inflation continued to rise for another  seven months after reaching nine percent  after this it took 21 months for  inflation to actually come back down  similarly in 1974 inflation continued to  rise for another 11 months after  reaching nine percent and it took 23  months after this for inflation to cool  back down so we could easily see  inflation continue to rise into q2 of  2023 from there it could take to q1 and  285 for inflation to go back down to 3  percent so we're by no means close to  the end but we may be close to the peak  the fed seems to finally be taking  inflation seriously so chances are good  that we see a peak by the end of this  year but it's still a long way down most  of 2023 would still be elevated  inflation even though it would be  declining and it wouldn't be until 2024  or early 2025 that we see normal levels  even then we wouldn't be out of the  woods though both times we saw high  inflation in the past 100 years we saw  multiple peaks in the 1940s we saw three  peaks and in the 1970s we saw two peaks  so if inflation starts heading back up  in 2025 or 2026 we're likely in for a  full decade of high inflation  in the end the fed expected inflation to  be transferred due to a recovering labor  market along with expectations for  demand to cool down as a result they  were willing to let inflation run haunt  for a little as the economy recovered  but the labor market would end up  recovering faster than they expected not  to mention it we also saw the supply  shortage the labor shortage and the  ukraine russia war which screwed up the  fed's plan significantly at this point  inflation has already skyrocketed to  over 9  and all we can do is wait for the fed's  tightening to take effect and for  shortages to slowly ease unfortunately  neither of these are fast processes and  we're likely looking at a minimum of  another year or two of elevated  inflation ahead of us even if things do  cool down fast we can't let our guards  down because history has shown us that  inflation often comes in the form of  multiple peaks so we would have to  continue staying cautious to avoid a  second or even third peak but that's  just what i think when do you guys think  inflation will peak comment that down  below  also drop a like if you wish your salary  was inflating as fast as gas prices and  of course consider checking out our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hurry and i'll see  you guys on the next one  [Music]

